WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:04.537
Bladesmiths, in your first
round of competition today,

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.573
you'll be using these.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:10.543
[dramatic music playing]

00:00:13.145 --> 00:00:15.014
On this table,
we've got ball bearings,

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:16.816
bandsaw blades,
bike chain,

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:19.452
we've got all manner
of high-carbon steel.

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:21.354
And in this first round
of competition,

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.790
you'll have to choose at least
three of the steels here

00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:27.627
<i> and combine them
 with 1095 powder</i>

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.128 line:0%
<i> into a canoe canister</i>

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:31.197 line:0%
<i> to make
 a canister Damascus billet</i>

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:33.566 line:0%
<i> from which you will forge
 a signature blade</i>

00:00:33.632 --> 00:00:34.967 line:0%
<i> in your signature style.</i>

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:36.101
Good luck.

00:00:36.168 --> 00:00:37.336
Your time starts now.

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:39.105
[dramatic rock music playing]

00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:41.974
We've got the Hardmans
versus the Harrisons over here.

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:44.277
Competing against each other

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.612
and then against each other
at the same time.

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:49.983
The potential biggest issue
for me in this challenge

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:52.485
<i> is I have never had
 a canister fail on me</i>

00:00:52.551 --> 00:00:54.920
<i> so I don't know what to do
 if it does.</i>

00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:59.492
I have done three canisters.
[laughs]

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:02.495
Ron's taking his billet
over to the drill press.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:04.396 line:0%
[Ben] Yeah, he might put
a vent hole in his canister.

00:01:04.496 --> 00:01:07.199 line:0%
If you want to be exacting,
do it the same way every time

00:01:07.332 --> 00:01:08.868 line:0%
and you'll get the same results.

00:01:09.368 --> 00:01:11.871 line:0%
She's going for drilling a hole
as well.

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.172
-[Doug] Same thing.
-[Wil] Oh man.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:14.474
This is the way
the family does it.

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:16.475
-[Doug] Yep.
-[Ron] Looking good.

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:17.710
Hot metal.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:20.413
-Ron comes off the press...
-Hot metal coming back.

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:23.817
...and like clockwork, Jessica's
there to take his place.

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.520
Okay, Jessica's
not really pressing

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:28.554
as much as she oughta be.

00:01:28.654 --> 00:01:30.590
I don't have enough weight
behind me

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:33.226
to actually make the bar go down
as much as I want it to go down.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:36.362
<i> I have to kind of rock myself,
 put my weight forward</i>

00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:38.464
and move back,
have to go forward again.

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:40.700
[Wil] I noticed Ron,
while she was doing that.

00:01:40.766 --> 00:01:43.135
Of course. You're watching over
your little apprentice.

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:44.470
Your own child.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.506
And either you're beaming
or you're concerned.

00:01:48.073 --> 00:01:49.775
[Ron]<i> I have a really good
 forge weld.</i>

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:53.245 line:0%
<i> I know that I'm gonna do
 an integrated bolster,</i>

00:01:53.378 --> 00:01:55.014 line:0%
<i> and so I'm setting that up.</i>

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.150 line:0%
<i> The reason I'm putting in
 the bolster</i>

00:01:57.216 --> 00:01:59.719 line:0%
<i> is because I've got
 such a long blade,</i>

00:01:59.785 --> 00:02:02.655
I want more weight to be
right back by the index finger.

00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:04.991
Jesse's profile's
coming along great.

00:02:05.058 --> 00:02:08.628
I wanna make sure I have holes
in my tang by end of the round,

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:11.898
<i>and drilling through hard steel
 is never a good option.</i>

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:14.934 line:0%
<i> So I've decided to go ahead
 and drift those holes in.</i>

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:18.404 line:0%
<i> Couple of heats,
had all three of my holes done.</i>

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:22.775
[Wil] Jessica's really gonna
have to ramp it up here

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:25.712
because she still just has
a rectangular billet.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:30.016
It's taking me slightly longer
to forge out my billet

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:31.450
<i> than I had hoped.</i>

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.486
I'm behind everybody
on the floor.

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:37.490
Hopefully, if I pace myself
right, I should be okay.

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:42.162
[dramatic music playing]

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:44.330
[Jesse]<i> I've got a small warp.</i>

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.365
There's nothing I can't
take off with a grinder.

00:02:46.465 --> 00:02:48.668
[Wil] Jesse really doing
a bang-up job.

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:50.770
[metallic whirring]

00:02:52.037 --> 00:02:53.373
[Ron]<i> I'm behind schedule,</i>

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.507
<i> and so I start to second-guess
 a little bit</i>

00:02:55.607 --> 00:02:57.309
the decision on putting
a bolster in there.

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.078
<i> There are risks
 with doing this.</i>

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:01.247
<i> This was not something
 the judges asked for.</i>

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:02.982
I don't know if they're
going to appreciate it.

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:06.986
[Wil] I like what Ron's doing,
putting in an integral bolster.

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:08.086
Super sexy pants.

00:03:08.187 --> 00:03:10.189
-How you doin'?
-Hangin' in there.

00:03:10.289 --> 00:03:12.525
-Time good?
-I think so.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:14.860
[Ron]<i> It's different in this
 environment with my daughter</i>

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.097
<i> because I'm not just thinking
 about my own thing in my head,</i>

00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:19.331
I'm also thinking about hers.

00:03:19.398 --> 00:03:21.100
I'm not interfering,
not doing anything,

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:22.835
but it's still in the back
of my head,

00:03:22.935 --> 00:03:24.103
"I wonder how she's doing."

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:25.738
[dramatic rock music playing]

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:27.807
[Wil] Look at Jessica
swing this hammer

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.208
like she wants the money.

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:31.077
Finally Jessica's
making a blade.

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:35.147
[Jessica]<i> My plan now is to be
 done forging in five minutes</i>

00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:37.116
and then have my holes
drilled for my tang,

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:38.851
<i> and then get it to grind,</i>

00:03:38.951 --> 00:03:40.552
<i> to quench, and get it edged.</i>

00:03:40.652 --> 00:03:42.288
There's a lot
of little steps between.

00:03:42.354 --> 00:03:44.991
A lot could go wrong,
but a lot could also go right.

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.561
-Looks awesome, Jessie.
-Thanks!

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.798
-Yeah, Dad. Woo!
-[David] Yes!

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:53.199
-All right.
-Nice.

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:55.000
[Ben] At this point,
both fathers have quenched

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.169
and neither child has quenched.

00:03:57.269 --> 00:03:59.872
[dramatic music playing]

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:01.741
[Wil] Whoa!

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:04.444
You didn't need that ponytail,
did ya, kid?!

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:07.880
Jessica's blade seems to be
very, very thick

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.849
and she has yet to grind down

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:12.851
to a high-carbon steel edge.

00:04:12.985 --> 00:04:15.020
Yeah, and then get it clean
enough so you can edge it.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.989
-Yep.
-[Doug] What I love

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:18.391
is the family dynamics here.

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:19.858
Because you're not
doing it alone.

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:21.828
There's a spiritual support
you're going through.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:24.330
I know my blade is too thick

00:04:24.463 --> 00:04:27.199
<i> so I try to hog some steel off
 real quick.</i>

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:30.402
Three, two, one!

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:32.705
Bladesmiths, pull your blades
out of the acid.

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:36.075
This first round of competition
is over!

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:38.144
[judges applaud]

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:41.447
Time's finally up. I don't know
whether to pass out or lay down.

00:04:41.513 --> 00:04:43.349
Bladesmiths, welcome
to the strength test.

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:45.317
To test the strength
and durability of your edge,

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:46.886
as well as the overall
construction of your blades,

00:04:47.019 --> 00:04:50.856
I will be bashing them
mercilessly into these poles.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:52.224
Ron, you're first. Ready?

00:04:52.324 --> 00:04:53.693
Absolutely. Have at it.

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:57.964
[dramatic rock music playing]

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:07.540
[music fades]

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:10.943
[tense music playing]

00:05:11.543 --> 00:05:14.647
Well, Ron, you've got
a pretty obtuse edge

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:16.582
which is good
at avoiding damage,

00:05:16.715 --> 00:05:18.283
not the best for fine cutting.

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:21.521
There's one tiny place here
that has glinting on it.

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:24.256
But other than that,
the edge is in good shape.

00:05:24.356 --> 00:05:26.392
One thing I don't like
about this knife

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:28.061
is right here.

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:29.762
It is round and small.

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:31.764
<i> And there was a couple times
 when I was hitting</i>

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:34.067
<i> where the blade would just
 twist in my hand.</i>

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:37.069
But it was comfortable.
It's just difficult to control

00:05:37.169 --> 00:05:38.304
because of the size of it.

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:39.806
Yes, sir.

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:42.308
Jessica, you see
what we're up to.

00:05:42.375 --> 00:05:43.508
-You ready?
-Yes, sir.

00:05:43.608 --> 00:05:47.245
[dramatic music slowly builds]

00:05:47.346 --> 00:05:49.849
[dramatic electronic music
playing]

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:03.062
-Good job.
-Thank you.

00:06:03.195 --> 00:06:04.363
[Ben] Well, Jessica...

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.799
there's a glint
that happened right there.

00:06:06.865 --> 00:06:08.968
The rest of your edge
held up really well.

00:06:09.101 --> 00:06:12.137
But there are sharp corners
here on the handle

00:06:12.237 --> 00:06:14.207
that were biting into my pinky.

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:16.541
But much less weight
than it had before.

00:06:16.641 --> 00:06:18.411
-So, well done.
-Thank you.

00:06:18.978 --> 00:06:21.147
Jesse, you're up.
How are you thinking?

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.415
Um... little nervous,
but have fun.

00:06:23.515 --> 00:06:26.152
[dramatic rock music playing]

00:06:36.863 --> 00:06:38.531
[music fades]

00:06:40.866 --> 00:06:43.435
Well, Jesse, I like
the profile you got here.

00:06:43.535 --> 00:06:45.537
It's got a pretty obtuse edge

00:06:45.637 --> 00:06:48.173
for everyday use,
but it makes a good chopper.

00:06:48.273 --> 00:06:50.343
So it held up well in this test.

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:52.511
Although it's a relatively
simple-looking handle,

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:53.779
it's a comfortable handle.

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:56.415
So, in all,
your edge held up well,

00:06:56.848 --> 00:06:59.051
it's a good-feeling
and good-working knife.

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:00.686
-Well done.
-Thank you.

00:07:01.587 --> 00:07:05.524
[dramatic music playing]

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:08.060
All right, gang, it's time
for the sharpness test.

00:07:08.161 --> 00:07:09.662
The apple slice.

00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:12.397
I'm looking for where the apple
is cut all the way through.

00:07:12.531 --> 00:07:14.300
Not cut partially through

00:07:14.433 --> 00:07:15.701
and broken the rest of the way.

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:17.636
-Ron, you're first. Ready?
-Absolutely.

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:20.807
[dramatic music playing]

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:29.381
[tense music playing]

00:07:29.448 --> 00:07:31.317
[David] Okay, Ron. So...

00:07:31.417 --> 00:07:33.486
pretty obtuse edge.

00:07:33.553 --> 00:07:37.556
And you can see that half
that apple is cut smoothly,

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:40.325
and then it cracks.
It's a fat edge.

00:07:40.459 --> 00:07:42.361
But will it cut?
Yes, it will cut.

00:07:42.428 --> 00:07:43.395
Gotcha.

00:07:43.962 --> 00:07:45.932
All right, Jessica,
you're next. Ready?

00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:47.066
-Yes, sir.
-Okay.

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:49.469
[dramatic music playing]

00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:07.886
[tense music playing]

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.023
So, Jessica, you can see
on this blade

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:13.659
that one side is almost flat

00:08:13.726 --> 00:08:15.494
and the other side
kinda curves down to it.

00:08:16.095 --> 00:08:18.530
So I start going through
the apples, just whoosh.

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:20.432
Like a wing.
Rode right up out of 'em.

00:08:20.532 --> 00:08:22.367
The biggest thing is,

00:08:22.467 --> 00:08:24.236
the square edges on this handle,

00:08:24.336 --> 00:08:26.072
I feel all these corners.

00:08:26.205 --> 00:08:27.740
But you did a good job

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.708
getting a lot of weight out
of the blade of this knife,

00:08:29.808 --> 00:08:32.245
and it will cut.
You got that going for you.

00:08:33.378 --> 00:08:35.114
All right, Jesse. You're up.

00:08:35.247 --> 00:08:36.348
-Ready?
-I think so.

00:08:36.415 --> 00:08:39.719
[dramatic music playing]

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.662
-Yeah, buddy.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:08:50.795 --> 00:08:52.631
[dramatic rock music playing]

00:08:53.131 --> 00:08:55.735
[David] So Jesse,
what I'm looking at here is

00:08:55.835 --> 00:08:57.970
actually some pretty clean cuts
on these apples.

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.105
Thank you.

00:08:59.205 --> 00:09:00.672
That's a good shape.

00:09:00.772 --> 00:09:02.942
Cut apples nicely. Good job.

00:09:03.042 --> 00:09:04.709
Thank you.

00:09:04.809 --> 00:09:06.345
[Wil] All right, Bladesmiths,

00:09:06.445 --> 00:09:08.247
the judges' deliberation
is complete.

00:09:08.314 --> 00:09:09.615
They've made
their final decision.

00:09:09.682 --> 00:09:12.151
It's time for one of you smiths
to leave the Forge.

00:09:12.818 --> 00:09:14.954
The Bladesmith
leaving the Forge is...

00:09:15.054 --> 00:09:17.323
[dramatic music playing]

00:09:19.424 --> 00:09:20.325
...Jessica.

00:09:20.425 --> 00:09:21.826
Your blade did not make the cut.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.430
Doug Marcaida's
gonna tell you why.

00:09:24.497 --> 00:09:26.532
Jessica, your edge
wasn't as sharp

00:09:26.665 --> 00:09:27.967
as that of your competitors,

00:09:28.034 --> 00:09:30.169
and your handle
had some hot spots

00:09:30.236 --> 00:09:32.771
that made it uncomfortable to
swing during the strength test.

00:09:32.871 --> 00:09:34.373
For that reason,
we're sending you home.

00:09:34.473 --> 00:09:35.474
Right.

00:09:35.608 --> 00:09:37.376
Jessica, you did
a fantastic job,

00:09:37.509 --> 00:09:40.346
but at this time, I have to
ask you to surrender your blade

00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:41.714
and leave the Forge.

00:09:42.815 --> 00:09:44.317
[Jessica]
<i> Obviously I'm disappointed</i>

00:09:44.383 --> 00:09:46.752
<i> but at the same time,
 I am thrilled for my dad.</i>

00:09:47.419 --> 00:09:50.022
I get to go back home and be
my dad's biggest cheerleader.

00:09:50.156 --> 00:09:52.525
<i> -Take it for the Hardmans.</i>
-[Wil] Aw.

00:09:52.658 --> 00:09:54.660
This competition came down
to the details.

00:09:54.727 --> 00:09:56.962
When I get home, I'm gonna
give my family a big hug

00:09:57.029 --> 00:09:59.599
<i> and go hang out with my dogs.</i>

